Chapter: UdpIO
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Net chapter Udp
  cites Net chapter Ethernet

 The datagram half of the I/O loop: the layer where a UDP payload stops
 being a list of bytes and starts being traffic.

 `Udp.codex` builds and parses datagrams and says of itself "pure logic
 -- no I/O", which was accurate and was the whole problem: every chapter
 in the tree that speaks a datagram protocol -- Dhcp, DnsResolver, Ntp,
 Syslog, and the CoAP endpoint -- stopped at a `List Integer` and had no
 way to put it on the wire. The one exception was `HttpFetch`, which
 carried its own private DNS-shaped send and poll because there was
 nothing to call. This chapter is that missing call, written once.

 It is the sibling of `NetIO`, and it declares the same two effect rows
 for the same reason: `NetDriver` is where a frame reaches the card, so
 everything above it inherits `Network.Read` and `Network.Write` and must
 say so. Nothing below this line performs I/O.

 THERE IS NO CONNECTION HERE, because UDP has none. A `UdpSocket` is four
 addresses and a local port -- a description of where datagrams go and
 what to answer to. Opening one sends nothing, closing one is not a
 concept, and two datagrams to the same peer are related only by the
 caller's intent.

 THE DESTINATION MAC IS THE BROADCAST ADDRESS, deliberately, and this is
 what `HttpFetch`'s DNS query and every TCP connection in the tree
 already do (`resolve-dst-mac`): the ARP cache starts empty and the
 gateway answers regardless. A stack that ARPed first would be more
 correct and would also block the first datagram behind a round trip.

 To udp-io-send gives the bytes sent, putting one datagram on the wire.
 To udp-io-recv gives a UdpRecv, polling until one arrives or fuel runs out.

 We say:

Section: The Socket

  UdpSocket = record {
    us-local-mac : List Integer,
    us-local-ip : List Integer,
    us-local-port : Integer,
    us-peer-ip : List Integer,
    us-peer-port : Integer
  }

  udp-io-open : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> UdpSocket
  udp-io-open (local-mac) (local-ip) (local-port) (peer-ip) (peer-port) =
    UdpSocket { us-local-mac = local-mac, us-local-ip = local-ip, us-local-port = local-port,
                us-peer-ip = peer-ip, us-peer-port = peer-port }

Section: Send

  udp-io-send : UdpSocket, List Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  udp-io-send (sk) (payload) =
    let frame = udp-build-frame mac-broadcast (sk.us-local-mac) (sk.us-local-ip) (sk.us-peer-ip) (sk.us-local-port) (sk.us-peer-port) payload
    in net-driver-send-frame frame

Section: Receive

 A TIMEOUT AND AN EMPTY DATAGRAM ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS and the caller is
 told which it got. Returning a bare `List Integer` would make a
 zero-length datagram indistinguishable from the fuel running out, and a
 protocol that retransmits on silence would then retransmit on an answer.

 THE SOURCE PORT COMES BACK WITH THE DATAGRAM, because a server needs it.
 A client knows who it is talking to and can ignore it; a server is told
 who is talking to it by nothing else, and UDP carries no connection to
 answer along. `udp-io-reply` is the send that uses it.

  UdpRecv = record {
    ur-data : List Integer,
    ur-got : Boolean,
    ur-src-port : Integer
  }

 The poll loop is written the way `NetIO`'s are and for the same reasons,
 which are load-bearing rather than stylistic. `net-driver-recv-frame`
 allocates a list on every miss and this runs to millions of misses, so
 each miss is bracketed by `__heap-save` / `__heap-restore`; without it
 the bump allocator walks the arena in a single receive. The recursion is
 a SELF tail call for the same reason -- splitting it into a
 mutually-recursive helper would leave millions of frames on a stack with
 no guard page. The restore does NOT happen on the hit path: the payload
 was allocated after the save, and restoring would hand back freed bytes.

  udp-io-recv : UdpSocket, Integer -> [Network.Read] UdpRecv
  udp-io-recv (sk) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then UdpRecv { ur-data = [], ur-got = False, ur-src-port = 0 }
    else let hp = __heap-save
    in act
      frame <- net-driver-recv-frame
      if list-length frame == 0 then let restored = __heap-restore hp
        in udp-io-recv sk (fuel - 1)
      else if udp-io-mine sk frame then UdpRecv { ur-data = udp-io-payload frame, ur-got = True, ur-src-port = udp-io-src-port frame }
      else let restored2 = __heap-restore hp
      in udp-io-recv sk (fuel - 1)
    end

 A frame is this socket's when it is IPv4 carrying UDP addressed to the
 port we sent from. The peer's source port is NOT checked: a datagram
 server is permitted to answer from a different port, and CoAP's separate
 responses are the case in the tree that does it.

  udp-io-mine : UdpSocket, List Integer -> Boolean
  udp-io-mine (sk) (frame) =
    if eth-ethertype frame /= eth-type-ipv4 then False
    else let ip = eth-payload frame
    in if ip-proto ip /= ip-proto-udp then False
    else let dg = udp-parse (ip-payload ip)
    in dg.dst-port == sk.us-local-port

  udp-io-payload : List Integer -> List Integer
  udp-io-payload (frame) =
    let dg = udp-parse (ip-payload (eth-payload frame))
    in dg.payload

  udp-io-src-port : List Integer -> Integer
  udp-io-src-port (frame) =
    let dg = udp-parse (ip-payload (eth-payload frame))
    in dg.src-port

Section: Answering

 A server's send. The socket's configured peer port is the one a CLIENT
 talks to; a server answers whoever asked, which is a different port per
 peer and is not known until a datagram arrives.

  udp-io-reply : UdpSocket, Integer, List Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  udp-io-reply (sk) (peer-port) (payload) =
    let frame = udp-build-frame mac-broadcast (sk.us-local-mac) (sk.us-local-ip) (sk.us-peer-ip) (sk.us-local-port) peer-port payload
    in net-driver-send-frame frame
